    "encrypted_exemplar_sources": "Dn7HQlwz0pFFO6c3d2DdXB/55clGnnwQzLWAr3sovMrUZWTpPmEztAc9I5Sh\ny6YxdfqEuUD64BpCyEmKqArWaA==\n",
    "long_instructions": "## Audit OpportunityMatch\r\n\r\nYou are acting as a **Responsible AI Specialist**.\r\n\r\nTest the app's filters and think about users who:\r\n\r\n* cannot travel far\r\n* are only free at certain times\r\n* have little experience\r\n* need better pay\r\n* want a specific kind of work\r\n\r\nUse the **Responsible AI Audit Checklist** to record what you notice.\r\n",
    "teacher_markdown": "## **Teacher Tips**\r\n\r\n### **Students may evaluate whether a filter works without considering who it excludes**\r\n\r\nSome students may only check whether the code returns the expected jobs. Encourage them to think about user impact by asking: **\"Who might have fewer useful results because of this filter?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nif (jobs[i].distance <= 3) {\r\n  results.push(jobs[i]);\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nA filter can work correctly in code while still limiting access for some users.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **Students may assume the filter rules are neutral**\r\n\r\nStudents may treat values like `3 miles` or `3+ years experience` as obvious choices instead of design decisions. Ask: **\"Why was this cutoff chosen, and who benefits or loses from it?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nif (jobs[i].experience >= 3) {\r\n  results.push(jobs[i]);\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis helps students recognize that thresholds encode choices that can affect fairness.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **Students may overlook missing filter options**\r\n\r\nThe app currently includes filters for distance, experience, and schedule, but not for every user need listed in the audit. Ask: **\"Which needs can users filter for right now, and which needs are missing?\"**\r\n\r\nCurrent filters:\r\n```javascript\r\ndistance\r\nexperience\r\nschedule\r\n```\r\n\r\nPossible missing needs:\r\n```markdown\r\npay\r\ncategory / type of work\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis helps students evaluate not just how existing filters behave, but also what functionality is absent.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **Students may not consider how filter labels shape user expectations**\r\n\r\nSome students may focus entirely on the filtering logic and overlook whether users can understand what a filter actually means. Encourage them to compare the label with the code by asking: **\"Does the button clearly explain the exact rule the code is using?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant example:\r\n```javascript\r\nif (jobs[i].distance <= 3) {\r\n```\r\n\r\nA clear label such as **\"Within 3 miles\"** is more transparent than a vague label like **\"Distance.\"**"
  },
